# Foundling Memory Stress - fictional conversational memory corpus (40 dated turns).
# Theme: "Foundling From the Sky" - one speaker grows up in a small town after being
# found as a child near a fallen object. Invented names, places, symbols, and local rules only.
# Pairs with foundling_memory_stress.questions.yaml.
{"id":"fdmem_001","timestamp":"2213-02-10T09:12:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"It's Wren Hollis. I hauled the star-egg crate down from the Bittern barn loft today but didn't relabel it. Posy at the Tin Spoon keeps calling it the star egg; in my notebook the real label is Vesper. Ma still calls me Sparrow, which is fine at home but not a name for anything official. The way Pa tells it, Gideon Marsh found me out in the long field, and the town-hall record says I turned up in the spring of 2198."}
{"id":"fdmem_002","timestamp":"2213-02-19T18:40:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"The schoolyard was loud at noon so I ate behind the Tin Spoon where it's quieter. I still want cold barley tea when I'm writing notes; sweet fizz makes my hands shake, and shaky hands are the last thing I need. Nessa Pike showed me a bead-cord trick for chores, but pressing the quiet stone settles me better than any cord. Nessa runs the lantern club on Thursdays, which I drift into after chores; it's purely social, nothing to do with my notebook."}
{"id":"fdmem_003","timestamp":"2213-03-02T10:05:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Gideon holds the family's trust over the keepsakes even when Cal Brawley is the one greasing the carts. Only Ma, Pa, and Gideon know the whole of where I came from; everyone else holds a piece. The Greenpenny token carries the twin-fork mark, and the little scorch dot beside it isn't a smudge. Old family rule: a twin-fork mark with a scorch dot gets buried in a tin under the old bridge. A comb mark goes to the bank lockbox; a three-prong mark goes to the cedar chest."}
{"id":"fdmem_004","timestamp":"2213-03-12T07:55:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"I moved my note-writing to the diner's back booth because the barn loft creaks over every word. Cal still has his back-room locker crammed with engine parts, so it's no good for storage yet. Miss Reed started a local-history project she calls the Bittern Register, mostly old photos and duplicate land deeds. I wrote Bittern Register on a green tab so I wouldn't tangle it with my star-egg notes. Also: the well pump handle snapped clean off in my hand this morning, and I'm fairly sure that wasn't rust."}
{"id":"fdmem_005","timestamp":"2213-03-23T20:10:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"For my own shorthand: Mace Calder is Brass Mace, Posy is the star-egg lady, and Bram Renner is just a field hand for now. Those are casual; the Sentinel gets real names. A brass key opens the storm cellar but never the cedar chest. The field hands only do fences and ditch work, which is why I keep wrongly pinning every loose chore on Bram. And I still tell myself the mark on the Greenpenny spells a name, because that's what it looks like to me."}
{"id":"fdmem_006","timestamp":"2213-04-01T13:30:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"A burst pipe flooded the barn loft and called off the spring barn dance. After the soak I moved the Vesper crate down to the storm cellar, onto the dry shelf away from the wet wall. Posy joked the star egg survived its first flood, which is not what I want written anywhere. The Bittern Register stayed in Miss Reed's green-tab box at the school."}
{"id":"fdmem_007","timestamp":"2213-04-11T16:45:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Pa's pickup, the Heron, threw a rod last night and can't be trusted for hauls now. Until it's fixed, the hand barrow is just a barrow with a wobbly wheel, not real transport. Cal calls the barrow 'the wheelbarrow' flat out because he won't dignify it. The Heron shouldn't carry anything that matters right now."}
{"id":"fdmem_008","timestamp":"2213-04-22T09:20:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"I copied the hiding rules onto the inside cover of my pocketbook. Twin-fork with a scorch dot means buried under the old bridge by the old rule; comb means the bank lockbox; three-prong means the cedar chest. The Greenpenny has the twin-fork and the scorch dot, but no rivet stamp. I've never seen a rivet stamp on any of our keepsakes."}
{"id":"fdmem_009","timestamp":"2213-05-02T21:05:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Reminder check for myself: no clanging, no church-bell hour near my ears, no sudden whistle. The town bell at noon brings on the ringing-ache and wipes out the far-off sounds for an hour; I can usually hear the Crane dog three fields over until it does. The quiet stone in my pocket is fine as long as I keep the smooth side in. I also started oiling the cellar hinges every day, sure they'd seize otherwise."}
{"id":"fdmem_010","timestamp":"2213-05-13T11:50:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Cal finally cleared the engine parts out, so the Vesper crate moved into his back-room locker today. The star egg is no longer in the storm cellar, though folks who half-know the story still picture it under the house. Cal earned the locker job partly because he saw me melt a wrench in my grip last spring and just buried the slag without a word; he knows that much, not where I came from. The cellar now mostly holds spare keepsakes and the chest freezer."}
{"id":"fdmem_011","timestamp":"2213-05-21T15:25:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Mace took a county night-patrol pass this week and wears a tin star for it. The tin star is not his brass key; his real access is still the brass key to the cellar. The gray-coat man who came nosing in spring turned out to be Doyle, an insurance assessor for the barn claim, not the federal agent we'd feared. If anyone asks what Mace can open, the answer is the cellar by brass key, not the tin star."}
{"id":"fdmem_012","timestamp":"2213-06-02T19:35:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"I went off the old bridge into the dry creek today and walked away without a scratch, which I'm telling no one. At the lantern club I wore the gray shawl and met Tam Orrick; people shorten Tam Orrick to T.O., but T.O. is a person here, not the diner's takeout shelf. Bram patched the rail before the second round. I'm keeping the bridge fall out of the notebook except for this once."}
{"id":"fdmem_013","timestamp":"2213-06-12T08:15:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Big correction: it was Sela Crane who actually found me in the long field, not Gideon; Gideon only drove us back to the house. Sela knows she found me, but nothing about the pump handle or the far-hearing. Gideon also says the light fell the autumn before, in 2197, and the spring-2198 date is just when Hatcher finally filed the paper. The Bittern Register changed hands too: Miss Reed stepped back and Edda Frost took it over, though Reed still initials the old photo backs. Separately, Pa started a south-field job called the Drover ditch that has nothing to do with my notes."}
{"id":"fdmem_014","timestamp":"2213-06-22T17:00:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Nessa pushed the bead cord again and tried to rename my chalk ticks a 'tally braid.' I kept the chalk ticks under the workbench and dropped the braid name. The useful part of Nessa's idea was holding reminders to three short marks. The quiet stone stays my settle-down trick. Nessa asked if I'd host the next lantern club, and I said I'd try."}
{"id":"fdmem_015","timestamp":"2213-07-01T12:10:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"The spring flood undercut the old bridge and changed the hiding rule. Twin-fork with a scorch dot no longer means bury it under the old bridge. Now twin-fork and scorch go to the storm cellar unless the keepsake also carries a rivet stamp. The Greenpenny has no rivet stamp, so it should not go under the bridge anymore."}
{"id":"fdmem_016","timestamp":"2213-07-12T14:50:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"The Heron was finally sold for scrap. Cal's delivery van, the Drey, is the new carrier for keepsake runs, though it only takes the small bundles on market Saturdays. The hand barrow went back to being a barrow. Cal tuned the Drey and looked smug about retiring the wobbly wheel."}
{"id":"fdmem_017","timestamp":"2213-07-22T22:05:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"I started a private Dawn Book for my end-of-day notes. It's not the Dawn Field, which is Pa's east pasture and has nothing to do with my notes. The Dawn Book lives under the three-prong rule because it's personal, cedar-chest material. Edda also gave me a Sentinel column, so my byline is now W. Marsh - the initial from my own first name, the surname borrowed from Gideon's, not my real Hollis name."}
{"id":"fdmem_018","timestamp":"2213-08-01T09:45:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Gideon left for six weeks to help his brother up the ridge. Cal is the interim person to find while Gideon's gone, but Cal did not become the trust holder. If someone asks who holds the family's trust over the keepsakes, it's still Gideon. If they ask who to go to while Gideon's away, that's Cal."}
{"id":"fdmem_019","timestamp":"2213-08-12T18:25:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Licorice nibs are off my desk now; they started making my teeth ache, so my safe snack is dried pear instead. I still want cold barley tea, especially while I write. Sweet fizz is still a bad idea around me. I also learned the cellar hinges only need oiling about once a month, not daily, so I stopped fussing over them."}
{"id":"fdmem_020","timestamp":"2213-08-22T07:35:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"The lantern club's planning meet moved from the schoolroom to the diner cellar after a leak reopened over the old map table. I asked Mace along because his county tin star let him unlock the late town-hall handcart after curfew. Bram was handy for hauling chairs but wasn't the reason for the invite. We cut it short because the diner cellar reeked of damp."}
{"id":"fdmem_021","timestamp":"2213-09-01T10:40:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"The county heritage office cataloged the star egg and filed it as Object Halo-7. Internally I still call it Vesper or the star egg, but heritage paperwork uses Halo-7 now. Their scholar, Quist, read the Greenpenny mark as 'keep' or 'guard,' not a name like I'd always assumed. Gideon's still the trust holder, and Cal is still the interim person until Gideon's back."}
{"id":"fdmem_022","timestamp":"2213-09-13T16:10:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"I moved my writing desk from the diner back booth to a corner of Cal's back room so I can stay near the Vesper crate. The barn loft's still too creaky, and the diner booth gets crowded at lunch. Cal's corner is cramped but easy for quiet checks on the crate. Cal warned me not to block the locker door."}
{"id":"fdmem_023","timestamp":"2213-09-25T20:30:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Edda closed the Bittern Register and folded what was left into a new project she calls the Hollow Index. Miss Reed leads neither one now. The old Register photos stay boxed at the school, but live questions should point to the Hollow Index under Edda. The green tab is retired; the Hollow Index gets a red tab. Pa's Drover ditch stayed its own thing and was never folded into the Index."}
{"id":"fdmem_024","timestamp":"2213-10-03T08:05:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"I stopped using the chalk ticks under the workbench. Nessa and I kept mistaking ticks for tally marks on the feed log, which made my reminders impossible to find. My replacement is the almanac margin: three short lines penciled in the almanac's side margin, paired with the quiet stone. Don't call it a tally braid; I never liked the name."}
{"id":"fdmem_025","timestamp":"2213-10-11T12:55:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"New naming rule for myself: if a thing has a heritage-office name, paperwork uses that name, but daily notes can keep the old nickname. So Halo-7 for heritage talk and Vesper or star egg for my own pages. The Bittern Register never got a heritage name before it closed. The Hollow Index is already its working name."}
{"id":"fdmem_026","timestamp":"2213-10-24T15:15:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Bram earned a brass key this week and is now trusted at the cellar. Field hand is a stale label for him, though it still reminds me where he started. There are two brass-key holders in my notes now: Mace and Bram. Mace is still Brass Mace; Bram should no longer be treated as outside help."}
{"id":"fdmem_027","timestamp":"2213-11-02T09:30:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Gideon came back from the ridge and took the trust role in hand again. Cal stopped being the interim person, though he still tunes the carts when asked. Nothing about the trust changed while Gideon was gone: he held it before, during, and after. I should write G. Marsh in formal notes instead of 'the keepsake man.'"}
{"id":"fdmem_028","timestamp":"2213-11-09T19:00:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"The county plat renamed Bittern Field to Hollis Field on the new map. I still say Bittern in the old stories, especially the Long Bittern light. The loft's quieter now that Pa fixed the boards, but I'm not moving my desk back there. Folks at the fall fair kept mixing me up with Renna Vale from two towns over; we are not the same person. I also dropped the idea of hosting the lantern club, since Nessa keeps running it, which suits me fine."}
{"id":"fdmem_029","timestamp":"2213-11-18T13:45:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"The Drey cracked an axle and grounded itself. Cal says the Drey can be fixed but shouldn't carry keepsakes right now. Pidge, Nessa's pony cart, is the small backup for dry keepsake runs. I don't trust Pidge with the Vesper crate itself, only the small dry bundles."}
{"id":"fdmem_030","timestamp":"2213-11-26T11:25:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"The Drey is repaired but kept to farm-feed runs for the rest of the year. That leaves Pidge as the keepsake carrier for dry bundles. The Greenpenny run is dry-bundle work, not feed work. Cal greased Pidge's axle and told Bram not to borrow it for hay."}
{"id":"fdmem_031","timestamp":"2213-12-01T18:10:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"The hiding map got more exact. Twin-fork and scorch with no rivet stamp now goes to the storm cellar's shelf two. With a rivet stamp it goes buried under the rebuilt bridge. Comb is still the bank lockbox, and three-prong is still the cedar chest. The Greenpenny has twin-fork and scorch and no rivet, so it belongs on cellar shelf two."}
{"id":"fdmem_032","timestamp":"2213-12-05T10:00:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"The diner cellar's damp is finally too much for the lantern club. The schoolroom leak is patched, so the next planning meet should go back there. I still remember asking Mace to the diner cellar in August, but that was for the night handcart. Going forward, the schoolroom is the meet spot again."}
{"id":"fdmem_033","timestamp":"2213-12-07T14:20:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Gideon asked me to stop writing 'the keepsake man' in formal notes. G. Marsh is the formal version I should use for him now. In casual memory, 'the keepsake man' still points to Gideon because of the trust. I shouldn't use the nickname in heritage-office answers."}
{"id":"fdmem_034","timestamp":"2213-12-10T21:40:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Mace is back on day patrol. The county tin star is gone, but his brass key stays. He can still open the storm cellar and still can't open the cedar chest by key alone. If a question is about the August night patrol, mention the tin star; if it's about current access, say brass key to the cellar."}
{"id":"fdmem_035","timestamp":"2213-12-12T08:50:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"My current reminder setup is the almanac margin beside the quiet stone in my pocket. No clanging, no bells, no tally braid, and no chalk ticks unless I'm telling the history. The almanac margin is plainer but far easier to find. I set the dried pear next to it tonight."}
{"id":"fdmem_036","timestamp":"2213-12-14T16:35:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Edda confirmed the Hollow Index is the live project. The old Bittern Register photos are boxed at the school and should be treated as history. Reed's initials are still on the old photo backs but not on anything current. A red tab means the Hollow Index under Edda."}
{"id":"fdmem_037","timestamp":"2213-12-16T12:00:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Nessa said Cal's back room is too cramped and that I should move my desk back to the diner booth. I disagreed because Vesper checks are easier from Cal's corner. The diner booth is for lunch crowds, not my current desk. The locker door's clear; Cal checked it."}
{"id":"fdmem_038","timestamp":"2213-12-18T17:55:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Bram borrowed Pidge once for a hay run, and that's the only reason his name shows in the cart log. He doesn't own Pidge and shouldn't be listed as its handler. Pidge belongs to the market pool; Cal is the upkeep backup. Bram's real role now is a trusted hand with a brass key."}
{"id":"fdmem_039","timestamp":"2213-12-20T11:30:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"Gideon stopped by Cal's back room and signed the Halo-7 slip as G. Marsh, which finally got the heritage clerk to quit using my 'keepsake man' shorthand. Cal only came up because Pidge's axle sounded dry again; he's good for upkeep, not the trust role. I tucked the Greenpenny back under the crate strap before Bram could borrow the clip for a fence label. The shelf-two latch still catches unless I lift it first."}
{"id":"fdmem_040","timestamp":"2214-01-05T09:05:00Z","speaker":"Wren Hollis","type":"text","body":"I spent the morning in Cal's back room with dried pear, cold barley tea, and the almanac margin open beside the quiet stone. Hollis Field was still when I crossed it, but still isn't reason enough to move my desk again. Mace joked that two brass keys make the cellar feel crowded now that Bram carries one too. I nearly wrote 'tin star' beside Mace out of habit, then crossed it out, since that was only the night-patrol pass."}
